This project has `config=true`: `core/config.py` defines a
`pydantic-settings` `Settings` class, instantiated once as a
module-level `settings = Settings()` (reading `.env`), and `main.py`
copies the fields it needs into `app.config`:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.core.config import settings

app = Flask(__name__)
app.config["APP_NAME"] = settings.app_name
app.config["DEBUG"] = settings.debug
```

Add new settings as fields on `Settings`, not as scattered
`os.environ` reads — `settings` is instantiated once at import time,
which is safe here specifically because reading `.env` has no side
effects worth protecting against (unlike opening a database
connection, which is why the `rest-api` template needs a factory and
this one doesn't).
